During an attempt to reproduce C343-81, I ran into an unrelated
grammar bug.  The what-is command (/) will tell you that the ghost
you have selected is "A Hether's ghost on a wall" or "A Michiel's
ghost on the floor of the room" or what have you.

Some languages use definite articles in conjunction with possessives
(Greek does, for example -- "the David's ghost" or even "the David's
the ghost" are word-for-word renderings of legitimate noun phrases
in Greek), but English is not such a language, and I am not aware of
any language that uses an indefinite article together with a
possessive in this manner.

